---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ironThumos } from '../data/iron-thumos';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebPage',
  '@id': 'https://www.cody-peterson.com/iron-thumos/#webpage',
  url: 'https://www.cody-peterson.com/iron-thumos/',
  name: `${ironThumos.title} — Cody Peterson`,
  about: { '@id': `https://doi.org/${ironThumos.doi}` },
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.cody-peterson.com/#person' },
};
---
<Base title="The Iron Thūmos and the Empty Vessel" description="Cody Peterson’s open-access Jung Journal essay on Homer, endurance, and Jung’s Answer to Job. Read the published article or download the PDF at Taylor & Francis." jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <header class="page-header essay-header">
    <div class="page-header-inner essay-title-page">
      <div class="essay-title-main">
        <h1>The Iron Thūmos and the Empty Vessel.</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">{ironThumos.subtitle}</p>
        <p class="essay-byline">Cody Peterson · <em>Jung Journal: Culture &amp; Psyche</em>, 20(3), 103–117</p>
      </div>
      <div class="essay-title-side">
        <figure class="epigraph">
          <blockquote class="greek" lang="grc"><p><span class="colon">τέτλαθι δή, κραδίη·</span> <span class="colon">καὶ κύντερον ἄλλο ποτʼ ἔτλης.</span></p></blockquote>
          <figcaption><span class="epigraph-translation">“Endure now, my kradiē; you endured something even more dog-like than this before.”</span><cite><em>Odyssey</em> 20.18, trans. Cody Peterson</cite></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="hero-actions"><a class="text-link" href={ironThumos.fullText}>Read the essay free</a><a class="text-link" href={ironThumos.pdf}>Download PDF at Taylor &amp; Francis</a></div>
        <p class="source-note">Published online 9 September 2026. The full text and PDF are freely available from the publisher.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="section paper-section" aria-labelledby="essay-question">
    <div class="section-inner research-intro">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">A way into the essay</span><h2 id="essay-question">Why would Odysseus turn down immortality?</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Calypso offers Odysseus a life without aging or death. He chooses the dangerous journey home. That choice opens a question at the heart of this essay: what becomes possible for the soul because a human life has limits?</p>
        <p>I follow Homer’s language of feeling and endurance into a conversation with Jung’s <em>Answer to Job</em>. The argument turns on three conditions of mortal life—permanent loss, radical uncertainty, and utter powerlessness—and on what the soul may acquire through bearing them.</p>
        <p>From Odysseus and Achilles, the essay moves to the Incarnation, Gethsemane, and Golgotha. It asks what the divine can learn through entering the conditions of a mortal life.</p>
        <p>You can begin with the published essay, or use <a href={ironThumos.companion}>the short reading companion on Seba</a> to orient yourself to its central questions.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={ironThumos.fullText}>Continue at Taylor &amp; Francis</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-alt" aria-labelledby="essay-citation">
    <div class="section-inner research-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">Publication</span><h2 id="essay-citation">Cite the essay.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Peterson, Cody. 2026. “The Iron Thūmos and the Empty Vessel: A Homeric Response to ‘Answer to Job.’” <em>Jung Journal: Culture &amp; Psyche</em> 20 (3): 103–117.</p><p><a href={`https://doi.org/${ironThumos.doi}`}>doi:{ironThumos.doi}</a></p><p class="source-note">Published under a Creative Commons Attribution–NonCommercial–NoDerivatives 4.0 license.</p><a class="text-link" href="/research/#publications">All publications</a></div></div>
  </section>
</Base>
